{
  "dom_id": "step-q6657",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-2x-1?$",
  "worked_solution": "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-1$ and the leading coefficient is $2.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of\u00a0}-1}{\\text{factor of\u00a0}2}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $2$ does not take the above form. The value $2$ can be interpreted as a fraction $\\frac{2}{1},$ and the numerator $(2)$ is not a factor of $-1.$",
